{
  "id": "ts-301",
  "name": "TypeScript Advanced — Conditional & Mapped Types",
  "description": "Conditional types, mapped types, `infer`, and template literal types — the type-system power tools",
  "cells": [
    {
      "id": "ts-301-md-1",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "source": "# TypeScript 301 — Type-System Power Tools\n\nThis notebook covers the advanced type-level features that let you express *transformations* between types:\n\n1. **Conditional types** — `T extends U ? X : Y`\n2. **Mapped types** — iterate a type's keys and rebuild a new type\n3. **`infer`** — pattern-match within a conditional and bind a name\n4. **Template literal types** — string types you can build by interpolation\n5. **Key remapping** — `as` clauses in mapped types",
      "output": "",
      "executed": false
    },
    {
      "id": "ts-301-1",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "typescript",
      "source": "//@ anchor: conditional-types\n//@ description: T extends U ? X : Y\n\n// A conditional type is an if/else that runs in the TYPE system, at compile time.\n// Read `T extends string` as \"is T assignable to string?\" - not as inheritance.\n// The whole expression evaluates to a type, never to a value.\ntype IsString<T> = T extends string ? \"yes\" : \"no\";\n\n// These are resolved while type-checking; nothing here exists at runtime.\ntype A = IsString<\"hello\">;  // \"yes\" - a string literal type IS a string\ntype B = IsString<42>;       // \"no\"\ntype C = IsString<boolean>;  // \"no\"\n\n// Distributive - applied to each union member\n// When a naked type parameter meets a union, the conditional DISTRIBUTES: it is\n// applied to each member separately and the results are unioned back together.\n// `never` is the empty type, and unioning with never drops the member - which is\n// how this filters. (Wrapping T in a tuple, [T] extends [string], switches the\n// distribution off, if you ever want it evaluated as a whole.)\ntype OnlyStrings<T> = T extends string ? T : never;\n// \"hello\" is absorbed into the broader `string`, so the result simplifies.\ntype Filtered = OnlyStrings<string | number | boolean | \"hello\">; // \"hello\" | string\n\n// The only reason these constants exist is to give the cell something to print;\n// the types above were already checked before any of it ran.\nconst exA: A = \"yes\";\nconst exB: B = \"no\";\nconst exC: C = \"no\";\nconst f:   Filtered = \"barista\";\n\nconsole.log({ exA, exB, exC, f });",
      "output": "",
      "executed": false,
      "anchor": "conditional-types",
      "description": "T extends U ? X : Y"
    },
    {
      "id": "ts-301-2",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "typescript",
      "source": "//@ anchor: mapped-types\n//@ description: Rebuild types by iterating their keys\n\ninterface User { id: number; name: string; email: string; }\n\n// Make everything optional (TypeScript's built-in Partial does exactly this)\n// A mapped type is a for-loop over a type's keys. `keyof T` yields the union\n// \"id\" | \"name\" | \"email\"; `[K in ...]` walks it; `T[K]` is an INDEXED ACCESS\n// that looks up the property type for that key. The `?` adds optionality to\n// every rebuilt property.\ntype MyPartial<T> = { [K in keyof T]?: T[K] };\n\n// Make everything readonly\n// Same loop, different modifier. Prefixing with `-` removes a modifier instead:\n// `-readonly` and `-?` are how Required<T> and Mutable<T> are written.\ntype MyReadonly<T> = { readonly [K in keyof T]: T[K] };\n\n// Turn every property into a string version of itself\n// The value type need not depend on K at all - here every property becomes\n// `string` regardless of what it was, which is the shape of form or query data.\ntype Stringify<T> = { [K in keyof T]: string };\n\n// Only `name` supplied, and that is fine because every key is optional.\nconst p: MyPartial<User>    = { name: \"Bob\" };\n// Readonly is checked on WRITE, not on construction, so building it is fine but\n// `r.id = 2` afterwards would be an error.\nconst r: MyReadonly<User>   = { id: 1, name: \"A\", email: \"a@b.c\" };\n// id is \"1\", a string - which is exactly what Stringify demanded.\nconst s: Stringify<User>    = { id: \"1\", name: \"A\", email: \"a@b.c\" };\n\nconsole.log({ p, r, s });",
      "output": "",
      "executed": false,
      "anchor": "mapped-types",
      "description": "Rebuild types by iterating their keys"
    },
    {
      "id": "ts-301-3",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "typescript",
      "source": "//@ anchor: infer-keyword\n//@ description: `infer` extracts a type from a position in another type\n\n// `infer U` says: \"match this shape, and wherever U sits, capture what was\n// there and name it U\". It is pattern matching for types, and it is only legal\n// inside the `extends` clause of a conditional type.\ntype UnpackArray<T> = T extends (infer U)[] ? U : T;\n\ntype A = UnpackArray<number[]>;   // number\ntype B = UnpackArray<string[]>;   // string\ntype C = UnpackArray<boolean>;    // boolean (passes through)\n\n// Built-in ReturnType is just `infer R` over the return position\n// The pattern matches any function; `...args: any[]` deliberately accepts every\n// parameter list, because only the return position is of interest here.\ntype MyReturnType<F> = F extends (...args: any[]) => infer R ? R : never;\n\n// No return annotation, so the inferred type is { id: number; name: string;\n// createdAt: Date }.\nfunction makeUser(id: number, name: string) {\n  return { id, name, createdAt: new Date() };\n}\n// `typeof makeUser` crosses from the value world into the type world, giving the\n// function's type; MyReturnType then pulls out what it returns. Derive the type\n// from the implementation and the two can never fall out of step.\ntype User = MyReturnType<typeof makeUser>;\n\nconst u: User = makeUser(1, \"Alice\");\nconsole.log(u);",
      "output": "",
      "executed": false,
      "anchor": "infer-keyword",
      "description": "`infer` extracts a type from a position in another type"
    },
    {
      "id": "ts-301-md-2",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "source": "## Template literal types\n\nString types you can build by **interpolating** other string types. Great for API routes, CSS units, and ID prefixes.",
      "output": "",
      "executed": false
    },
    {
      "id": "ts-301-4",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "typescript",
      "source": "//@ anchor: template-literal-types\n//@ description: CSS units, route prefixes, event names\n\ntype CssUnit  = \"px\" | \"em\" | \"rem\" | \"%\";\n// A template literal TYPE: the same backtick syntax as a string value, but\n// operating on types. `${number}` matches any numeric literal, so this describes\n// every string of digits followed by one of the four units - a set far too large\n// to enumerate by hand, expressed in one line.\ntype CssValue = `${number}${CssUnit}`;\n\n// Both are checked against the pattern. \"100\" or \"24pixels\" would be rejected.\nconst width:  CssValue = \"100%\";\nconst height: CssValue = \"24px\";\n\ntype HttpMethod = \"GET\" | \"POST\" | \"PUT\" | \"DELETE\";\n// `P extends string` CONSTRAINS the parameter so it can be interpolated. When a\n// union is interpolated the result is the cross product, so Route<\"users\"> is\n// four types: \"GET /api/users\" | \"POST /api/users\" | and so on.\ntype Route<P extends string> = `${HttpMethod} /api/${P}`;\n\nconst r1: Route<\"users\">         = \"GET /api/users\";\n// A typo such as \"PUTT /api/users/:id\" or a missing /api prefix would not\n// compile - the route string is validated by the type system, not at runtime.\nconst r2: Route<\"users/:id\">     = \"PUT /api/users/:id\";\n\nconsole.log({ width, height, r1, r2 });",
      "output": "",
      "executed": false,
      "anchor": "template-literal-types",
      "description": "CSS units, route prefixes, event names"
    },
    {
      "id": "ts-301-md-3",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "source": "## Key remapping with `as`\n\nA mapped type can rewrite the keys themselves — useful for building getter signatures or renaming a whole shape.",
      "output": "",
      "executed": false
    },
    {
      "id": "ts-301-5",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "typescript",
      "source": "//@ anchor: key-remapping\n//@ description: Build Getters<T> from a record type\n\ninterface Point { x: number; y: number; z: number; }\n\n// For every key K in Point, produce a `get{K}` method that returns the value.\n// The `as` clause renames each key as it is mapped. Two pieces do the work:\n// Capitalize<...> is a built-in intrinsic that uppercases the first character,\n// and `string & K` narrows K to the string case, since keyof can also include\n// number and symbol keys, which Capitalize cannot accept.\n// The value type `() => T[K]` keeps each getter's return type tied to its own\n// property, so getX returns number because Point[\"x\"] is number.\ntype Getters<T> = {\n  [K in keyof T as `get${Capitalize<string & K>}`]: () => T[K];\n};\n\ntype PointGetters = Getters<Point>;\n// PointGetters is { getX: () => number; getY: () => number; getZ: () => number }\n\n// Nothing generates this object - it is hand-written, and the type only checks\n// it. Rename a field on Point and this implementation stops compiling, which is\n// the point: the interface and its accessors stay in lockstep.\nconst impl: PointGetters = {\n  getX: () => 1,\n  getY: () => 2,\n  getZ: () => 3,\n};\n\nconsole.log(\"x =\", impl.getX(), \"y =\", impl.getY(), \"z =\", impl.getZ());",
      "output": "",
      "executed": false,
      "anchor": "key-remapping",
      "description": "Build Getters<T> from a record type"
    }
  ],
  "metadata": {
    "category": "tutorial",
